  "verbalization": "Sodium lauryl sulfate appears to interact with Riboflavin synthase through a multi-step mechanism involving the enzyme Potassium-transporting ATPase subunit beta. This interaction is supported by two distinct pathways, both of which lead to the same target, Riboflavin synthase. The first pathway involves Perforamone B, a compound that acts as a target of the Potassium-transporting ATPase subunit beta, and the second pathway involves a more complex, structurally distinct compound that also targets the same enzyme. The shared involvement of Potassium-transporting ATPase subunit beta in both pathways suggests a potential common mechanism of action, possibly through the inhibition of the enzyme or its regulatory functions. The evidence is consistent across different compounds, indicating that the interaction may be broadly relevant to Riboflavin synthase. The mechanism is further supported by the presence of multiple compounds that target the same enzyme, suggesting a potential role in metabolic pathways or cellular processes involving riboflavin synthesis. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that Sodium lauryl sulfate may exert its effects on Riboflavin synthase through a mechanism involving the Potassium-transporting ATPase subunit beta, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact nature of the interaction.",
